module lib/hf/scf_loop
  The Hartree-Fock self-consistent field (SCF) loop, written once for RHF,
  UHF and ROHF.

  Hartree-Fock replaces the repulsion between electrons by an average: each
  electron moves in the field of the charge cloud of all the others. That
  field depends on the orbitals, and the orbitals depend on the field, so the
  equations are solved by iteration. Guess a density, build the field (the
  Fock matrix) from it, solve for new orbitals, fill the lowest ones with
  electrons, and repeat until the density stops changing.

  RHF carries one total density, UHF one density per spin, and ROHF one
  density per spin with one shared set of orbitals. The three differ in how
  many densities the loop carries, in the matrix it diagonalizes (ROHF builds
  an effective Fock matrix, lib/hf/coupling) and in how orbitals are filled.
  Every stage that has more than one way to be done (guess, orthogonalizer,
  eigensolver, J/K builder, accelerator, ROHF coupling) is picked by a field
  of the run's options, so one loop runs every combination the tests check.
  The defs that pick (`gs_of`, `orth_of`, `eig_of`, `jk_of`,
  `accel_extrapolate`) call each member by name, so the default options run
  the production members directly.

scf_iterate  @297271d8
  how   Work in an orthonormal basis built from S, where each iteration is an
        ordinary symmetric eigenproblem. Each pass builds J and K from D,
        forms F, computes the energy, and measures how far F and D are from
        commuting. When the energy has stopped changing and the commutator is
        small, stop. Otherwise extrapolate F from the recent iterations (DIIS;
        PySCF leaves the guess's Fock matrix out, `diis_start = 1`),
        diagonalize it, and fill the lowest orbitals to get the next D.
  why   The converged density is a fixed point of "build F from D, solve,
        fill", and at that fixed point F and D commute (`F D S - S D F = 0`),
        which is the condition for the energy to be stationary. Iterating the
        map directly can oscillate or crawl; extrapolating with DIIS reaches the
        same answer in fewer iterations (lib/hf/rhf times both on water).
        Each iteration allocates what it computes; the compiled loop frees it
        when the iteration ends, so a long run uses no
        more memory than a short one (lib/hf/rhf runs past convergence to
        check it). The loop computes only what the next iteration needs: the
        energy change, the commutator norm and the DIIS history size it
        reports, and the checks that F is symmetric and that each density
        holds its electrons, are its debug lens (lib/hf/scf_loop.debug).
  D      density matrices in the AO basis, `[nd, n, n]`: `D[i, j] = sum over
         occupied orbitals of c_i c_j` (times two for a closed shell), where c
         holds an orbital's coefficients on the basis functions. `Tr(D S)` is
         the number of electrons in D.
  F      Fock matrices, `[nd, n, n]`: the one-electron Hamiltonian H plus the
         average repulsion, `F = H + J - K/2` for a closed shell. Its
         eigenvectors are the orbitals; it is symmetric.
  X      orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`: `X^T S X = I`. Its columns are combinations
         of basis functions that are orthonormal; `m < n` when nearly dependent
         combinations are dropped.
  JK     J and K of each density, `[2 nd, n, n]`. J is the classical repulsion
         from the charge cloud of a density; K, exchange, has no classical
         counterpart and acts only between electrons of the same spin.
  e      the energy of the current density, `E_nuc + (1/2) sum_d Tr D_d (H + F_d)`
  comm   the commutator step's products: the matrix DIIS extrapolates, its
         error, its largest element (`comm.norm`, zero exactly at
         self-consistency), and the densities in the orthonormal basis
  diis   the recent matrices and their errors that DIIS mixes
  Fnext  the matrix about to be diagonalized: DIIS's mix, level shifted when a
         shift is set
  eigs   its eigenvalues and eigenvectors, one set per matrix
  U      `S X`, `[n, m]`, which moves each new density into the orthonormal basis
  incr   the incremental Fock build's full-rebuild interval, `0` when every
         build uses the whole density (`scf_incremental`); it drops to `0`
         when a build from a change of density first meets the convergence
         test, because the noise those builds carry (about 1e-11 Eh on
         (H2O)8 6-31G*, from the quartets screening drops) is the size of the
         test itself
  Dbuilt the densities the last J and K were built from
  since  how many builds from a change of density since the last one from
         the whole density
  setup    the setup: H, S, the integrals, electron counts, options
  D0       the starting densities
  n        the number of basis functions
  nd       the number of densities the loop carries: `1` for RHF, `2` otherwise
  opts     the options as the array the registry members read
  eprev    the energy of the previous iteration
  conv     `1` when the loop stopped because it converged
  it       the iteration running, from 1; after the loop, how many ran
  r        the result record

  orthonormal_basis  build the orthonormal basis X from S, its m kept columns
  start              start the DIIS history, the incremental build's state and the loop's counters from the starting densities
  repeat for at most max_iter iterations, leaving once the energy changed less than e_tol and the commutator is below grad_tol
    coulomb_exchange   build J and K from the current densities: from the change since the last build, or from the whole densities every incr iterations
    fock               form the Fock matrices
    energy             compute the energy of the current densities
    commutator         move F and D into the orthonormal basis and measure how far they are from commuting
    diis               store the matrix and its error, and mix the stored ones by DIIS; in the first diis_start iterations, pass the new matrix on unmixed
    if a level shift is set
      level_shift        raise the empty orbitals by the shift
    diagonalize        diagonalize each matrix
    new_density        fill the lowest orbitals to make the next densities
  result             the result record: energy, orbitals, densities, the convergence record
  return it

scf_energy2  @769e0dc8
  how   `E_nuc + Tr(D H) + (1/2) Tr(D J) - (1/4) Tr(D K)` for one total
        density; for two spin densities, `E_nuc + Tr(Dt H) + (1/2) Tr(Dt Jt)
        - (1/2) sum_s Tr(D_s K_s)`, with `Dt` and `Jt` the totals over spins.
  why   The two formulas share no code after J and K are built, so their
        agreement catches a Fock matrix assembled wrongly
        [test "a converged density is the plain step's fixed point, and the energy formulas agree"].
  H     the core Hamiltonian, `[n, n]`
  JK    J and K of each density, `[2 nd, n, n]`: J of density d at `2 d`,
        K at `2 d + 1`
  D     the densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  enuc  the nuclear repulsion, hartree
  Da    the (alpha or total) density, transposed, so `sum(Da * H)` is `Tr(D H)`
  Db    the beta density, transposed
  eh    `Tr(Dt H) + (1/2) Tr(Dt Jt)`

scf_level_shift  @c33e52c2
  why   A larger gap between occupied and empty orbitals damps the swapping of
        orbitals between iterations, which is what makes some SCF runs
        oscillate. It changes the path, not the converged answer (the level
        shifted preset of lib/hf/rhf lands on PySCF's energy).
  setup  the setup, for the method
  Fnext  the matrices about to be diagonalized, `[k, m, m]`
  comm   the commutator step's products; its densities say which orbitals
         are occupied
  m      the size of the orthonormal basis
  shift  how far the virtual orbitals are raised, hartree
  w      electrons per orbital, so `P'/w` is a projector
  Fs     the shifted matrices, `[k, m, m]`
  d      the matrix of the stack
  Q      `I - P'/w` for matrix d, the projector on the empty orbitals
  a      a diagonal position

scf_result  @ebff9d3d
  why   Whenever the loop converged, the last J, K and Fock matrices were
        built from the densities it returns, so the energy, the commutator
        and both energy formulas describe that one state. The orbitals come
        from one more diagonalization of the last Fock matrices, so for RHF
        and UHF `D = C occ C^T`, which lib/hf/rhf checks on water.
  setup   the setup
  X       the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  D       the final densities, `[nd, n, n]`
  F       the Fock matrices built from the densities before D
  JK      their J and K
  e       the energy of those densities
  conv    `1` when the loop converged
  it      the number of iterations run
  opts    the options array
  method  the method the run used
  m       the size of the orthonormal basis, X's column count
  nd      the number of densities
  grad    the largest element of the AO commutator `F D S - S D F`
  d       a density
  s2      `<S^2>`, the expectation value of the total spin squared; `0` for RHF
  e2      the energy by the second formula (scf_energy2)
  half    half the RHF total density: the alpha density
  a       the alpha orbitals (RHF: the spatial orbitals)
  b       the beta orbitals
  total   the total density, alpha plus beta
  favg    the average of the two spin Fock matrices
  comm    ROHF's commutator products, for its effective matrix
  R       ROHF's eigensolver result
  V       its eigenvectors
  C       ROHF's shared orbital coefficients, `[n, m]`
  occ     ROHF's occupations: `2`, `1` or `0` per orbital

  convergence      the AO commutator, <S^2> and the energy by the second formula
  if the run is RHF
    closed_shell     the spatial orbitals, two electrons in each occupied one
    return the result
  if the run is ROHF
    shared_orbitals  the effective matrix's eigenvectors, occupied 2, 1 and 0
    return the result
  spin_orbitals    each spin's orbitals from its own Fock matrix
  return the result

scf_solve  @e4e5e35d
  why   ROHF with fewer alpha than beta electrons has the same open orbitals
        as the swapped problem; running that one and giving the spins back
        keeps the coupling code to one spin order
        [test "one entry point runs all three methods: the atom-free model gives 0.2 closed and 1.0 as a triplet"].
  setup   the setup
  start   the starting spin densities, `[2, n, n]`
  method  the method the setup carries, decided when it was made

scf  @a8014ec0
  setup  the setup
  n      the number of basis functions
  start  the guess's spin densities, `[2, n, n]`
  r      the result

  guess          make a starting density for each spin from the guess the options pick
  solve          run the loop from them with the method the setup asks for
  return the result

scf_step  @e0403a4d
  why   At a converged state the step gives the same densities back. That
        checks the Fock build, the eigensolver and the filling rule against
        PySCF one step at a time, without depending on the path an iteration
        takes [test "a converged density is the plain step's fixed point, and the energy formulas agree"].
  setup  the setup
  Da     the alpha density to step from, `[n, n]`
  Db     the beta density to step from, `[n, n]`
  plain  the options with one iteration, no accelerator, no level shift and
         a negative energy tolerance, so the loop never stops early

scf_setup  @4d2a9224
  why   One record carries everything the loop needs, so each stage member
        and each method takes the same argument, and the method is decided
        here, once.
  H     the core Hamiltonian `T + V`, `[n, n]`
  S     the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  G     the packed repulsion integrals, or the basis blob a direct J/K
        member reads
  na    the number of alpha (spin-up) electrons
  nb    the number of beta (spin-down) electrons
  enuc  the repulsion between the nuclei, hartree; a constant of the geometry
  o     the run's options (lib/hf/opts)

scf_setup_with  @c33a0125
  why   A record update (`ScfSetup { options: o, ..setup }`) keeps the method
        the setup was made with; a caller that changes the options' method,
        or anything the automatic choice depends on, rebuilds the setup here
        so the method is decided again.
  setup  the setup whose matrices, counts and nuclear repulsion are kept
  o      the new options

scf_method  @8b2ce83c
  why   A caller who leaves the method automatic gets the cheapest one that
        fits: one density for a closed shell, two for an open one. It is
        asked once, by `scf_setup`, and the setup carries the answer
        (`setup.method`), so the helpers of the loop read a field instead of
        deciding again; `scf_solve` asks it once more to catch a setup whose
        options or counts a record update changed.
  o     the run's options
  na    the number of alpha electrons
  nb    the number of beta electrons
  m     the method the options ask for, as a whole number

scf_opts  @435eeacb
  why   The registry members take a plain array rather than the options
        record, so a member of lib/hf/guess, eigen or accelerate reads its
        settings the same way whoever calls it.
  setup  the setup: options and electron counts

scf_weight  @60a561ec
  setup  the setup, for its method

scf_nocc  @0c964866
  setup  the setup, for its electron counts
  d     which density: `0` for the RHF total or alpha, `1` for beta

scf_incremental  @9a85d228
  why   A direct build costs less the smaller the density it is given,
        because its density screening skips the quartets that density does
        not reach; an in-core build costs the same whatever the density.
        So only the production direct member builds from the change of
        density, as PySCF's direct SCF does, and from the whole density
        every `fock_rebuild` iterations so the rounding of the sums cannot
        build up.
  setup  the setup, for its options

scf_nmatrix  @442be429
  setup  the setup, for its method
  nd    how many densities the loop carries

scf_swapped  @01d1b947
  why   ROHF's code assumes at least as many alpha as beta electrons; the
        other case runs as its mirror image (scf_solve).
  setup  the setup to swap

scf_commutator  @a46fa7e0
  why   At self-consistency F and D commute (`F D S = S D F` in the AO
        basis); the size of the commutator is the loop's convergence test
        and the error vector DIIS minimizes.
  setup  the setup, for S and the method
  X     the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  U     `S X`, which the loop computes once, `[n, m]`
  m     how many orthonormal functions X keeps: its column count
  F     the Fock matrices in the AO basis, `[nd, n, n]`
  D     the densities in the AO basis, `[nd, n, n]`
  opts  the options array, for ROHF's coupling member
  nd    the number of densities
  Fo    the Fock matrices in the orthonormal basis, `[nd, m, m]`
  P     the densities in the orthonormal basis (ROHF: the closed and open
        projectors), `[nd, m, m]`
  Fe    ROHF's effective Fock matrix, `[1, m, m]`
  E     the error matrices: the commutators, `[nd, m, m]`
  norm  the largest element of any error matrix
  d     the density being handled
  Pd    density d in the orthonormal basis
  Ed    its commutator with its Fock matrix

scf_diagonalize  @95287578
  why   The eigensolver is picked by `opts[OPT_EIGEN]` (`eig_of`), so
        every run can switch it or cross-check it.
  Fnext the matrices to diagonalize, `[k, m, m]`
  opts  the options array
  sh    Fnext's shape
  eigs  the results, one eigensolver record per matrix
  d     the matrix being diagonalized

scf_occupy  @eb987221
  why   Filling the lowest orbitals (aufbau) is how each iteration turns
        orbitals back into densities.
  setup  the setup: method and electron counts
  X     the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  eigs  the eigensolver results, one per matrix
  comm  the commutator step's products; ROHF reads its spin Fock matrices to
        choose the open orbitals
  m     the size of the orthonormal basis, X's column count
  V     ROHF's eigenvectors, `[m, m]`
  n     the number of basis functions
  nd    the number of densities
  D     the new densities in the AO basis, `[nd, n, n]`
  d     the density being filled

scf_coefficients  @bf7650c2
  why   The eigensolver works in the orthonormal basis; a caller wants the
        orbitals as combinations of the original basis functions.
  X     the orthogonalizer, `[n, m]`
  V     eigenvectors in the orthonormal basis, `[m, m]`

scf_aufbau  @f2eba142
  m     the number of orbitals
  nocc  how many are occupied
  w     electrons in each occupied orbital
  occ   the occupations, `[m]`

